# Vote on the Demand for an Industrial Department Grant
Tamil title: தொழில்துறை நிதி ஒதுக்கீட்டுக் கோரிக்கை (வாக்கெடுப்பு)

Speech 22, 8 July 1967

**Honourable C. N. Annadurai:** Mr Deputy Speaker, the Industries Minister will later explain the details of this grant, but members have raised general points to which I wish to respond. I say related points, not necessarily contrary ones.

Everyone agrees that industry must grow in this country. Differences concern what kind of industry should grow, in which sector, and who should enjoy its full benefit. Parties operate on the basis of those differences.

Some members appear disappointed that a newly responsible Government has not immediately launched great industrial schemes. We are united in wanting industrial growth, but in present circumstances a responsible Government's first task is to remove the economic and industrial stagnation already present.

Many previously healthy industries can no longer operate properly because they lack raw materials, face labour disputes, seek to divert capital elsewhere, suffer poor management, or encounter several of these causes together. We must first overcome this recession. Until then, starting new industries in either the private or public sector is difficult. The English economic term and policy quotation in Image 2 are destroyed by Tamil-only OCR; the recoverable Tamil clearly states that restoring existing industries and services is the immediate priority.

Opposition members say that we merely preserve what existed: if they started forty industries, we should start forty-five. That compels an examination of what happened during their term, though otherwise it would be unnecessary.

I personally hold former Industries Minister R. Venkataraman in high regard. But when members drag him into debate as a counter and praise him extravagantly, the opposite side must identify shortcomings. I therefore consider both sides his friends.

Statistics for recent years show what was established. The OCR records that in 1964–65 forty private-sector industries began, with none in the cooperative, State public, or Central public sectors. In 1965–66 it records twelve private, nine cooperative, no State-sector, and three Central-sector industries. In 1966–67 it records twelve private, two cooperative, no State-sector, and two Central-sector industries. The next year's label is damaged, but the readable totals are five private, none cooperative, five State-sector and two Central-sector industries. These figures require verification against departmental records.

That is the extent of growth achieved in those years. We could argue from opposite sides indefinitely. Better to begin with our shared desire for growth and propose industries that can now be established.

Former minister Poovaragan offered useful suggestions: build new industries from Neyveli waste and by-products, and expand the Virudhachalam ceramics factory despite its present stagnation. Experts studied the State's industrial resources years ago in Dr Lokanathan's report, which was tabled while I was formerly in the Assembly. We should revisit it. Not all its recommendations were implemented, and opportunities remain.

Tell us constructively to begin this or undertake that. Merely asking what we have done may express dissatisfaction that we came to power, but nothing more. Could we create another Perambur coach factory, Tiruchirappalli boiler plant, or Neyveli mine within our first hundred days? We have barely had time to excavate the files left before us. If you demand our achievements in a hundred days, I may have to show what you achieved in your first hundred. What we have done in a hundred days may exceed what you did in a thousand; old records will tell.

Mr Karuthiruman says all these industries arose only after independence, rather like saying, “I became clean only after I bathed.” We need not press even that claim too far. Perambur and Golden Rock already had railway works before independence; many Coimbatore mills and the Buckingham and Carnatic Mills also predated it. What is gained by claiming them?

After our term ends, compare what we achieved in five years with what you achieved. If you want us to leave midway, say so. But friends say we should remain, so comparison after the full term is proper.

Industrial development should be tested by three questions. Has industry grown in proportion to this State's natural resources? Does the State as a whole receive the resulting revenue? Are the developed industries under State Government authority?

Neyveli's natural wealth was developed by the Union Government. The Leader of the Opposition asks what difference it makes whether it belongs to the Centre or State. Everyone knows where the revenue goes. The State can seek to take over Neyveli when it wishes; I know the relevant details. Industrial development has not matched our natural resources and has occurred chiefly through the Centre, not under State supervision.

If we owned such enterprises, our people would gain employment and the State would gain non-tax revenue. We could then remove much of the taxation that burdens poor people. Examine the records and see how little industrial income the State Government has received. This is not an accusation against previous ministers; it reflects the industries available to the State.

The State's large undertakings are chiefly electricity and irrigation. Electricity struggles even to service interest on Government loans. If expected interest on irrigation capital is calculated, the OCR says there is a Rs 3 crore loss. Madras city transport operates at a loss, while services outside the city earn some profit, though not much. The quinine factory near Udhagamandalam has repeatedly faced closure; exports over the last two or three years produced only a modest profit.

Even without embracing State ownership as doctrine, everyone should agree that industrial development must be evaluated by the revenue it returns to the State.

Mr Poovaragan objected to my humorous suggestion that electric fans could be replaced by palm-leaf fans to create employment. I know the public well enough to trust that they distinguish a joke from policy. The point arose when members asked whether it was fair to send some employees home while demanding jobs for others. If employment alone were the criterion, switching off electric fans and employing people to wave palm fans would create more jobs. The public understands the distinction.

If jobs alone mattered, why object to the Centre running everything, or even to British owners opening new industries? They might create employment, but profits would leave the State. I am not saying the British and Union Government are identical. Yet the State also governs, needs revenue, and cannot tax people endlessly. Our natural wealth supports industry, but its revenue does not reach us.

No member believes the Union Government's treatment of the Salem steel plant is just. Salem iron in the Kanjamalai area has lain dormant since surveys were completed near the end of the nineteenth century, and we have discussed it for twenty years. It was expected in the Third Plan and omitted from the Fourth.

The Leader of the Opposition says courageously, “If they will begin it, let them; otherwise I shall.” It resembles telling a policeman, “Will you find the culprit, or shall I?” We say so only after losing confidence. His disappointment over the Centre's failure to take up Salem steel is greater even than mine, and members of his party share it, but they fear open criticism may create division within Congress.

I ask everyone to forget party divisions and unite at least on this issue. Uprising Day is an excellent opportunity. If responsible people like you participate, undesirable acts can be prevented or corrected. I repeat my invitation to join it.

The Government and people justly demand both the Salem steel plant and the Tuticorin deep-water harbour. Uprising Day will impress their benefits and necessity on the Union Government. It does not mean a nationwide shutdown or indiscriminate processions; we have no such plan. Every party should unite to insist that the Centre remedy its unjust treatment of these projects. I warmly invite members to relax party discipline enough to cooperate beyond party.

Industrial growth creates income, but revenue from Central enterprises goes to the Centre, while profits from private industries go to their owners. This helps explain the divergence between national income and per-capita income. As industry and total income rise, the benefit should spread so individual income rises too. A planned and just economy must narrow the disparity. In India, per-capita income has not risen in proportion to national income.

Dr Hande said industrial peace must first be protected and read a statement by the Kerala Government, asking why we had not similarly declared our industrial policy. Perhaps Kerala's Left Communist Chief Minister felt a need to dispel doubts about his party. We do not face the same need, but I shall clarify our policy.

First, remove industrial stagnation. Second, take every measure necessary for harmonious industrial relations. Third, progressively spread industrial income across society. Fourth, when new industries begin, arrange wherever possible for the resulting revenue to reach the State.

One may decorate these principles with words before or after and call them a manifesto, but that should not be necessary. The Government has decided to work on this basis.

Dr Hande also raised doubts about the proposed Arakkonam industry. He said it was not being placed where the original survey was conducted. His information is wrong: after proper investigation, action is being taken at that very site. He also questioned whether sufficient raw materials exist there. The project proceeds only after evidence established adequate supply.

He referred to a fifteen-page report by the Finance Secretary opposing it. I inquired because I wondered what could require such a report beyond indicating possible financial allocation. There are not fifteen pages, nor even fifteen lines, of adverse opinion from the Finance Secretary. The Industries Secretary and others are pursuing the project with great interest, and the Government is working to bring it into operation quickly.

The Industries Minister will answer the other details. I explained these principles only because Dr Hande said no industrial policy had been declared.

The Leader of the Opposition and others complain that we keep returning to what they failed to do over fifteen or twenty years. Even people writing a postcard begin with “Victory to Sri Rama,” though Rama's victory occurred long ago.

**P. G. Karuthiruman:** Do you believe that?

**Annadurai:** A good question. If I say no, they will go out this evening and tell the people Annadurai has no faith in Rama! (Laughter.) This is an old style. Even believers in the Ramayana know it is an imaginative story.

We cannot escape mutual references for a few years. If you end your attacks on us quickly, the habit will end quickly. If you begin every speech with “the Honourable Venkataraman,” we begin ours with “in the Honourable Karuthiruman's time.” If we agree the style has gone far enough, stop claiming the past and we shall not waste time pointing to it. We can attend together to present work.

Still, there is no wrong in examining records. If we identify a past error, you may prove it was not an error, and that proof will help us too. Do not react with disgust or irritation.

In the House, some speak with angry faces even when they feel no anger; once seated, they quietly smile. In Parliament, the respected Vajpayee was described by newspapers with an English epithet that the OCR has destroyed, yet when he laughed the building seemed to shake. Some consider a fierce manner part of speaking.

In these few months I have studied your state of mind. I am not merely a politician; I have experience in theatre and can recognise performance. Some members speak in whatever emotional mode the moment requires, and no more. Let no one take it amiss. I conclude by saying that I remain ready to receive superior ideas.

---NOTES---
The source identifies this as Speech 22 from the 8 July 1967 Assembly sitting, on voting for the Industrial Department grant. The OCR year “1067” is an unmistakable digit error; the scan context and companion Speech 21 establish 1967, while the Tamil source remains unchanged.

This is a separate speech from `080767.md`, not a continuation. Both retain their own speech numbers, subjects and bilingual files.

Tamil-only OCR destroyed several English expressions and quotations in Images 2, 6 and 13. Their recoverable Tamil context is translated, but no verbatim English is invented.

Annual sector counts, the Rs 3 crore irrigation-interest loss and other industrial statistics are OCR-derived source claims requiring verification against Assembly and departmental records.

“எழுச்சி நாள்” is rendered as Uprising Day, the all-party mobilisation proposed for the Salem steel plant and Tuticorin deep-water harbour; the speech explicitly distinguishes it from a general shutdown.
